1. Field Injection
-------------------
@Service
public class UserService {
    @Autowired
    private UserRepository repo;
}

Pros:
Less code.
Easy to write.

Cons:
Dependencies are hidden.
Harder to unit test.
Can't make the field final.
The object can exist in an incomplete state until Spring injects the dependency.

2. Constructor Injection (Preferred)
------------------------------------
@Service
public class UserService {
    private final UserRepository repo;

    public UserService(UserRepository repo) {
        this.repo = repo;
    }
}

Pros:
Dependencies are explicit.
Supports final fields.
Easier to write unit tests.
The object cannot be created without its required dependencies.

Cons:
Slightly more code (though modern IDEs generate constructors easily).


3. Setter Injection
-----------------------
@Service
public class UserService {
    private UserRepository repo;

    @Autowired
    public void setUserRepository(UserRepository repo) {
        this.repo = repo;
    }
}

Pros:
Good for optional dependencies.
You can change the dependency after object creation if needed.

Cons:
The object may be used before the setter is called.
Required dependencies are not enforced.
