fetchtype
@ManyToOne(fetch = FetchType.EAGER,cascade = CascadeType.ALL)

1. First Mapping (Recommended)
-----------------------------------
@OneToMany(cascade = CascadeType.ALL, fetch = FetchType.LAZY)
@JoinColumn(name = "countryId")
private Set<State> states;

Explanation
---------
Set<State> is a generic collection.
The collection can contain only State objects.
Type safety is provided at compile time.
FetchType.LAZY means states are loaded only when accessed.

Example:
---------
Country c = session.get(Country.class, 1);

// States are not loaded yet
System.out.println(c.getName());

// States loaded now
System.out.println(c.getStates());

Database query for states executes only when getStates() is called.


2. Second Mapping
--------------------
@OneToMany(cascade = CascadeType.ALL, fetch = FetchType.EAGER)
@JoinColumn(name = "countryId")
private Set states;

Problems
--------
Raw Set is used (no generic type).
Can store any object type.
Generates compiler warnings.
Not type-safe.

Example:
------------
Set states = new HashSet();
states.add(new State());
states.add("Hello");   // Allowed
states.add(100);       // Allowed

This can cause runtime issues.

Also:
--------
fetch = FetchType.EAGER


means Hibernate loads all states immediately whenever a country is loaded.

Country c = session.get(Country.class, 1);


Hibernate executes:
-----------------------
select * from country where country_id = 1;

select * from state where countryId = 1;


even if you never access states.


Best Practice
===============
@OneToMany(
    cascade = CascadeType.ALL,
    fetch = FetchType.LAZY
)
@JoinColumn(name = "countryId")

private Set<State> states = new HashSet<>();

Reasons:
-------------
Uses generics (Set<State>).
Type-safe.
Better performance with LAZY.
Avoids unnecessary database queries.


Real-world Example
--------------------------
Imagine opening a folder:

EAGER = Open folder and automatically load all 1000 files into RAM.
LAZY = Open folder, but load a file only when you click it.

That's why LAZY is generally preferred for @OneToMany and @ManyToMany relationships,
where the number of child records can be large.
